## The prompt is one synthetic user turn
The obvious construction — slice the parent's `MessageParam[]` up to the selection and append the framing turn — breaks the alternation the API requires whenever the selection lands in a user turn, and forces every thread to carry a "where does my visible transcript start" index into a shared array.
Instead a child thread's conversation is seeded with **exactly one** `MessageParam`: a user turn containing the parent transcript rendered as text, the verbatim selection, and the question. The whole conversation is re-uploaded every turn anyway (see `plans/2026-09-04-prototype-scaffolding.md`), so nothing is lost, and the seed is a pure `string`-producing function that unit tests can read.
The seed turn stays in the conversation forever — it is the first `MessageParam` and is re-sent on every turn, exactly like any other. `hiddenTurns` is purely a display concern: `messages` is already a projection of the turns for the UI, and this drops the first N from it. So the pane shows the assistant's answer first, the user never reads the framing we wrote on their behalf, and anchor offsets keep indexing the list the user can actually see, at every depth. This is the only change to `conversation.ts` beyond making `system` a constructor option.
"The task conversation up to the selection" means the parent's visible messages truncated after the message containing the selection's *end* — the agent should not see work that happened after the passage under discussion.
Context therefore accumulates down the tree rather than being replaced: a layer-1 thread's hidden seed already contains the task transcript, and a layer-2 thread's seed renders that thread — whose first *visible* message is the explanation — so the task context is still present, one level of quoting deeper. This is what makes "iterate down until the user gets it" work without any explicit context plumbing, and it is also the thing that will eventually blow up the prompt at depth. Fine for a prototype; the first symptom will be a long seed, not a wrong one.
## Highlights: many marks per message
`MessageView` stops taking one optional mark and takes a list. The message text is split at every mark boundary into segments; each segment is either plain or attributed to a thread, and an attributed segment is a clickable `<mark>` that dispatches `MARK_CLICKED`. Segments are rendered with `bindList` keyed by start offset.
